.233299,37.867413],[-108.235298,37.870312],[-108.236397,37.873314],[-108.237694,37.875912],[-108.247795,37.879513],[-108.250496,37.884514],[-108.253494,37.885914],[-108.2556,37.887913],[-108.257599,37.890411],[-108.2565,37.894211],[-108.2808,37.894413],[-108.357399,37.893612],[-108.366295,37.89381],[-108.390797,37.893512],[-108.437797,37.893912],[-108.455097,37.894212],[-108.500597,37.894212],[-108.587197,37.894812],[-108.624897,37.895312],[-108.678997,37.895212],[-108.740797,37.895412],[-108.804497,37.895412],[-108.817597,37.894812],[-108.818597,37.892012],[-108.818297,37.888912],[-108.820297,37.886012],[-108.820197,37.883312],[-108.820997,37.880912],[-108.875897,37.881012],[-108.893497,37.880812],[-108.899597,37.881012],[-108.929297,37.881212],[-109.041496,37.881212],[-109.0411,37.907211],[-109.041695,37.924713],[-109.042,37.943512],[-109.042793,37.962612],[-109.042999,37.974312],[-109.042097,38.153012],[-108.999597,38.153512],[-108.988497,38.153412],[-108.980497,38.153612],[-108.949397,38.153512],[-108.942097,38.153712],[-108.911297,38.153312],[-108.900197,38.154812]]]]},"properties":{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued October 28 at 2:42PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nLow pressure begins to dig southward from the PACNW on Friday,\ntapping into a fetch of Pacific moisture that will evolve into an\natmospheric river streaming into the Desert Southwest this weekend.\nShowers and thunderstorms develop on Friday and Saturday across the\nregion. Lighter rainfall totals Friday and Saturday will reflect,\ninitially lower, moisture. However, a few thunderstorms could produce\nimpactful downpours on saturated soils left over from last weekend's\nhistoric rainfall.\n\nDeeper moisture develops Saturday night and early Sunday. This is\nexpected to produce heavier and more widespread rainfall across\nsoutheast UT and southwest CO Sunday and Monday, mostly below 10000\nfeet. While rivers are forecast to remain below action stage, a\ngrowing concern lies in the potential for runoff around saturated\nhillsides in the region. This could produce rock, mud, and debris\nflows onto roadways, as well as flash flood prone burn scars, washes,\nslot canyons, and arroyos. Early estimates predict an area of 1 to 2\ninches of new rainfall for portions of southeast UT and southwest CO.\n\nThis is a preliminary forecast that hinges on slight shifts in a\nstrong moisture plume impacting the region. It also attempts to\ncapture the most statistically likely outcome, despite stronger and\nweaker outlier predictions. Outdoor enthusiasts, motorists, and\nresidents of recent flood impacted areas are encouraged to remain\nabreast of the developing forecast in the coming days.","ends":"2026-10-29T15:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.38c97534038bb54c112c5ff0215b743f983d5cfb.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.38c97534038bb54c112c5ff0215b743f983d5cfb.001.1"}}]}